<div class="b-logo">
    <div class="b-logo__roundel">
        {# The logo means "take me to the homepage", and the homepage is the front
           door — tunnel, clean slate. So it resets the conversation rather than
           returning you to one. data-wipe-thread is read by thread-memory.js on
           the click, because this and browser-back both land on `/` and have to
           mean opposite things. #}
        {# Two marks in the one link: the roundel, and an illustrated portrait that
           takes its place on hover / focus (crossfaded in _logo.scss). Both inline
           rather than in the spritemap: the portrait is built on a <mask> and a
           <clipPath>, and the sprite's svgo pass strips exactly those attributes
           (see vite.config.js). The portrait is decoration — aria-hidden, and the
           roundel keeps the accessible name — so a screen reader hears one logo. #}
        {# NAMED, not just titled. The link's accessible name would otherwise come from
   the roundel's own <title> ("Jon Leverrier Logo"), which names the picture
   rather than saying where the link goes — and it would disagree with the
   tooltip a sighted user sees. aria-label sets the link's name and title shows
   the same words on hover, so the two cannot drift apart. #}
        <a class="b-logo__link" href="{{ baseUrl }}"
           title="{{ siteName }}: Home" aria-label="{{ siteName }}: Home" data-wipe-thread>
            <svg class="b-logo__mark b-logo__mark--roundel" role="img" aria-labelledby="svg-logo" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="65" height="65" viewBox="0 0 65 65">
                <title id="svg-logo">{{ siteName }} Logo</title>
                <style>
                    .roundel-back {
                        fill:#FFFFFF;
                    }
                    .roundel-front {
                        fill:#162C41;
                    }
                </style>
